1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:26,699
While working at the FFTF you find yourself about to enter a confined space marked by this sign.

2
00:00:26,699 --> 00:00:32,579
The sign is there to inform you that there is a possibility of that space becoming oxygen

3
00:00:32,579 --> 00:00:48,539
deficient. Would you know what to do in this situation? In two breaths to you learn some

4
00:00:48,539 --> 00:00:54,820
general information about oxygen deficient spaces. If you walk into an oxygen deficient atmosphere

5
00:00:54,820 --> 00:01:00,259
you won't have a single warning and it's too late to hold your breath. You'll take two breaths and

6
00:01:00,259 --> 00:01:07,780
you'll collapse. In this presentation, we will look at two types of inert gas found in the FFTF

7
00:01:07,780 --> 00:01:13,039
which can displace oxygen, the precautions which are taken in the plant to cope with oxygen

8
00:01:13,039 --> 00:01:18,299
deficient environments, and some procedures which you should know when working in a space which

9
00:01:18,299 --> 00:01:25,899
could become oxygen deficient. Argon and nitrogen are inert gases. This means they have little

10
00:01:25,899 --> 00:01:31,379
or no ability to interact chemically with other substances. Because of this, they are

11
00:01:31,379 --> 00:01:38,859
used to separate or inert potentially volatile materials. Argon is used as a cover gas and

12
00:01:38,859 --> 00:01:47,900
as a buffer between seals in various places throughout the FFTF.

13
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:53,840
Since it is heavier than air, any argon that might leak may sink and concentrate in low-lying

14
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:56,039
areas.

15
00:01:56,039 --> 00:02:07,640
Nitrogen is used for valve actuation, to inert primary sodium cells, and to flood secondary

16
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:13,219
sodium cells in an emergency situation nitrogen is a light gas which mixes

17
00:02:13,219 --> 00:02:18,979
freely with air it may be found at all levels these gases are not in themselves

18
00:02:18,979 --> 00:02:23,719
toxic they are hazardous because they displace the oxygen which is necessary

19
00:02:23,719 --> 00:02:30,920
for life the normal amount of oxygen found in air is 21% the minimum amount

20
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:34,819
of oxygen necessary for a work atmosphere at Hedl has been established

21
00:02:34,819 --> 00:02:41,120
at 19.5 percent. This is very conservative since physical effects are not perceivable

22
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:48,840
above 16 percent oxygen. However, all monitors in use at the FFTF have their alarm set at

23
00:02:48,840 --> 00:02:56,459
the 19.5 percent level. Entry into a space with less than 19.5 percent oxygen requires

24
00:02:56,459 --> 00:03:03,780
use of approved respiratory protection and compliance with administrative procedure.

25
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:07,319
oxygen monitors are situated at the FFTF in the following

26
00:03:07,319 --> 00:03:12,060
locations. Areas where there is a high potential of an inert gas

27
00:03:12,060 --> 00:03:16,240
leak, such as a secondary sodium cell, and in areas where there

28
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:19,580
is little ventilation or air circulation. That is where the

29
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:24,219
air is changed less than one and a half times per hour. As

30
00:03:24,219 --> 00:03:28,580
previously stated, these monitors react to the 19.5%

31
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:32,879
oxygen level. When this level is reached, an alarm will go off.

32
00:03:33,780 --> 00:03:46,060
And if the monitor is located in a high noise area, a rotating beacon will also be activated.

33
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:51,759
In addition to having permanent oxygen monitors, all cells in the FFTF will be marked by this

34
00:03:51,759 --> 00:03:54,340
sign.

35
00:03:54,340 --> 00:03:57,979
Requirements for access to the space will be indicated on the sign.

36
00:03:57,979 --> 00:04:02,400
The degree of oxygen deficiency potential in the space will determine what actions are

37
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:04,500
required.

38
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:11,759
Common requirements include use of personal oxygen monitors, use of the buddy system,

39
00:04:11,759 --> 00:04:18,139
use of additional ventilation equipment, notification of the control room, and a five-minute escape

40
00:04:18,139 --> 00:04:21,180
pack available for immediate use.

41
00:04:21,180 --> 00:04:24,240
Let's take a closer look at these requirements.

42
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:29,639
There are two types of personal oxygen monitors in common use at the FFTF.

43
00:04:29,639 --> 00:04:33,639
The Gastek OX80 is the most commonly used.

44
00:04:33,639 --> 00:04:42,339
It is worn at waist level, and its alarm is a loud buzzing sound.

45
00:04:42,339 --> 00:04:50,019
When the Gastek is switched on, this alarm will sound briefly, to let you know it is

46
00:04:50,019 --> 00:04:52,019
operating properly.

47
00:04:52,019 --> 00:04:59,879
If the alarm continues, sounding intermittently, it is a signal that the battery is low and

48
00:04:59,879 --> 00:05:02,740
needs recharging.

49
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:08,439
To check oxygen levels in the space surrounding you, press the button near the readout window.

50
00:05:08,439 --> 00:05:12,180
The level will be shown in digital figures.

51
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:16,279
The Edmont-Wilson monitor is also worn at waist level.

52
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:23,100
This alarm is a high-pitched whistle.

53
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:28,520
If the alarm sounds when the Edmont Wilson is first turned on,

54
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:31,820
it is an indication the battery is low.

55
00:05:31,820 --> 00:05:35,880
The sensor for the Edmont Wilson is attached to an extension cord.

56
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:41,600
It may be lowered into a cell prior to entry in order to check oxygen levels.

57
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:47,439
Once again, these personal monitors have their alarms set at the 19.5 percent level.

58
00:05:47,439 --> 00:05:53,200
When an alarm goes off, take immediate action.

59
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:55,800
Oxygen monitors are your first line of protection.

60
00:05:55,800 --> 00:06:00,700
Believe what they tell you and make certain you know how to operate them.

61
00:06:00,700 --> 00:06:06,140
The buddy system is a well-known preventative measure, which has saved thousands of lives.

62
00:06:06,139 --> 00:06:11,800
In this instance, one person must remain outside the space and maintain either visual or verbal

63
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:15,219
contact with their partner.

64
00:06:15,219 --> 00:06:19,279
Additional ventilation for confined spaces may be provided by such equipment as portable

65
00:06:19,279 --> 00:06:22,800
blowers and flexible hose.

66
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:26,879
Another means of letting others know your whereabouts is to call the control room prior

67
00:06:26,879 --> 00:06:29,240
to entry.

68
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:34,439
If this procedure is posted, you must also notify the control room and confirm your departure

69
00:06:34,439 --> 00:06:40,379
you leave. Know when and how to use a five-minute escape pack. Before using,

70
00:06:40,379 --> 00:06:46,219
check the gauge on the unit to ensure it is charged properly. If an oxygen monitor

71
00:06:46,219 --> 00:06:52,899
alarm goes off, don the escape pack immediately. Open the case, pull out the

72
00:06:52,899 --> 00:07:03,740
unit, unfold the mask, pull the ring. If you hear a hiss, the unit is working

73
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:13,920
properly. Pull the mask over your head, adjust the next seal, and then the drawstring. The yellow tab

74
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:20,240
tightens and the red tab loosens. An important point to remember is that the escape pack is to

75
00:07:20,240 --> 00:07:28,139
be used for escape only. It is not to be used for rescue purposes. In an emergency situation,

76
00:07:28,139 --> 00:07:30,959
your job is to get out and warn others.

77
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:36,560
Don't become a victim yourself by trying to rescue someone without the proper equipment.

78
00:07:37,439 --> 00:07:42,019
Call the control room, and the emergency response team will initiate a rescue action.

79
00:07:42,019 --> 00:08:02,199
The most important aspect of safe entry into a confined space is your knowledge of safety

80
00:08:02,199 --> 00:08:09,519
procedures. When you are to work in a confined space, remember argon and nitrogen may cause a

81
00:08:09,519 --> 00:08:16,319
hazard by displacing oxygen in the air. Permanent oxygen monitors are situated in all normally

82
00:08:16,319 --> 00:08:22,500
accessible spaces, which might become oxygen deficient. Signs are posted at the entrances

83
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:28,680
to all confined spaces, listing requirements for entry. Heed the requirements and, if you

84
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:34,340
are wearing a personal oxygen monitor or five-minute escape pack, know how and when to use them.

85
00:08:34,340 --> 00:08:41,300
When an oxygen monitor alarm sounds, immediately don the five-minute escape pack, leave the

86
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:49,120
area and notify the control room.

87
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:52,660
You are the most important part of any safety program.

88
00:08:52,660 --> 00:08:57,259
Your knowledge and use of safety procedures will go a long way in helping to keep the

89
00:08:57,259 --> 00:08:59,139
FFTF a good place to work.
